 ## Overview
-Back-face culling for ray casting is an optimization technique that determines the entry and exit points of rays into a volume by rendering the back faces of the volume's bounding box to a texture, thereby avoiding the need to compute ray-cube intersections analytically in the raycasting shader [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It builds on [Coarse Mesh Traversal with Dual-Ray Culling](987-coarse-mesh-traversal-with-dual-ray-culling.md) by applying the principle of using front/back face distinction to a simple bounding box primitive, offloading geometry processing to the GPU rasterizer [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike single-pass methods that compute intersections in the fragment shader, this approach uses a two-pass rendering pipeline: the first pass captures the back-face depth/position, and the second pass uses this data to initialize the ray march, skipping empty space outside the volume [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+Back-face culling for ray casting is an optimization technique that determines the entry and exit points of rays into a volume by rendering the back faces of the volume's bounding box to a texture, thereby avoiding the need to compute ray-cube intersections analytically in the raycasting shader [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It builds on [Coarse Mesh Traversal with Dual-Ray Culling](987-coarse-mesh-traversal-with-dual-ray-culling.md) by applying the principle of using front/back face distinction to a simple bounding box primitive, offloading geometry processing to the GPU rasterizer [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike single-pass methods that compute intersections in the fragment shader, this approach uses a two-pass rendering pipeline: the first pass captures the back-face depth and position, and the second pass uses this data to initialize the ray march, skipping empty space outside the volume [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
 
